-- Opening a bank account is not just paperwork - before a bank can take
-- on a new customer, the law requires it to check that person against
-- watchlists. Two lists matter most here. A "sanctions" list names
-- people and entities the bank is forbidden to do business with. A "PEP"
-- list names politically exposed persons - senior officials and their
-- close associates - who are not forbidden but carry heightened risk and
-- need extra scrutiny. This whole discipline is known as KYC, "know your
-- customer".
--
-- Screening is not a one-time stamp. A clean result has an expiry, after
-- which it must be refreshed. And the lists themselves change: a name
-- that was clean last month can be flagged tomorrow when a list updates.
-- When a screening turns up a possible "match" against a list, that is
-- not an automatic rejection - real-world names collide, so a human
-- analyst has to adjudicate whether it is a genuine hit or a false
-- positive. Until that adjudication happens, the customer must not be
-- onboarded. A missed alert here is a real regulatory breach, not a
-- cosmetic bug.
--
-- Here is how Morpholog thinks about this. It does not store "facts" -
-- it stores *claims* it has agreed to admit because they obey the rules.
-- Two kinds of rule do the work below:
--
--   * an `invariant` is a rule that must hold for all admitted state,
--     forever. The runtime refuses to commit any change that would break
--     one. The rule that an onboarded customer must have clean, current,
--     unflagged screenings is an invariant - so onboarding the wrong
--     customer is not "discouraged", it is structurally impossible.
--   * a `require` is a gate on a single action, checked at the moment you
--     act and never again - for instance, refusing to register a
--     customer who already exists.
--
-- The screening decision itself - does this name actually match anyone
-- on the watchlist? - happens outside, at an external provider. The
-- bank's own state stays inside Morpholog; the provider's answer comes
-- back as a separate step that records the result, and only then do the
-- invariants gate onboarding.

program kyc_sanctions_screening

-- The vocabulary. Each `predicate` names a kind of claim this world can
-- hold: a registered customer, a screening request against a named list,
-- the result that came back, a pointer to the screening that currently
-- counts for each customer-and-list, a match waiting for an analyst, and
-- the onboarded standing itself.
predicate Customer(customer_id: Subject)
predicate Screening(screening_id: Subject, customer: Subject, list_type: Subject, requested_on: Date)
predicate ScreeningResult(screening_id: Subject, disposition: Subject, completed_on: Date, expires_on: Date)
predicate CurrentScreening(customer: Subject, list_type: Subject, screening_id: Subject)
predicate MatchUnderReview(screening_id: Subject, raised_on: Date)
predicate OnboardedCustomer(customer: Subject, onboarded_on: Date)


-- Intents are messages the system sends to the outside world once a
-- change commits, and each one routes to a different place: a screening
-- request goes to the external provider, a raised match goes to the
-- analyst review queue, an onboarding goes to core banking, a rejection
-- goes to compliance reporting. Declaring them up front, by name, means
-- a mistyped message cannot silently vanish into a queue nobody reads.
intent ScreeningRequested(screening_id: Subject, customer: Subject, list_type: Subject)
intent MatchRaised(screening_id: Subject, customer: Subject, list_type: Subject)
intent CustomerOnboarded(customer: Subject, onboarded_on: Date)
intent CustomerRejected(customer: Subject, reason: Subject)


-- The rules that must always hold. If any change would leave the world
-- violating one of these, the whole change is rejected at commit.

-- For each customer and each list, only one screening can be the
-- "current" one at a time. When a fresh screening arrives it takes over
-- as current and the old pointer is removed, so there is never any doubt
-- about which result counts right now.
invariant at_most_one_current_screening_per_customer_and_list_type:
    (CurrentScreening(c, t, s_a) and CurrentScreening(c, t, s_b)) implies (s_a = s_b)

-- An onboarded customer must have a current sanctions screening that
-- came back clean (or was cleared by an analyst after review) and that
-- has not yet expired as of the onboarding date. No clean, in-date
-- sanctions result, no onboarding.
invariant onboarded_requires_current_clean_sanctions:
    OnboardedCustomer(c, on_date) implies (
        CurrentScreening(c, #sanctions, s)
        and (ScreeningResult(s, #clean, completed, expires)
             or ScreeningResult(s, #adjudicated_clear, completed, expires))
        and (on_date on_or_before expires)
    )

-- The same rule again for the PEP list. Sanctions and PEP are separate
-- lists with separate legal weight, so a clean sanctions result does not
-- cover the PEP obligation - both must be current and clean.
invariant onboarded_requires_current_clean_pep:
    OnboardedCustomer(c, on_date) implies (
        CurrentScreening(c, #pep, s)
        and (ScreeningResult(s, #clean, completed, expires)
             or ScreeningResult(s, #adjudicated_clear, completed, expires))
        and (on_date on_or_before expires)
    )

-- An onboarded customer cannot have any unresolved match on any of
-- their screenings - not only the current one. If a re-screen turns up a
-- possible hit, that blocks onboarding even if an older clean result is
-- still the current one. A flagged match has to be settled by an analyst
-- (cleared as a false positive, or confirmed and the customer rejected);
-- it can never be quietly buried under a newer clean result. That is why
-- this rule looks across every screening the customer has, not just the
-- current pointer.
invariant onboarded_requires_no_unresolved_match:
    OnboardedCustomer(c, _) implies not (
        Screening(s, c, _, _) and MatchUnderReview(s, _)
    )


-- The ways state may change. A transformation proposes a set of
-- changes; the runtime applies them only if every invariant still holds
-- once they are in place. `admit` adds a claim, `retract` removes one,
-- `require` is a gate the action must pass first.

-- Register a new customer. The gate refuses to register someone who is
-- already on file - registering twice is turned away rather than quietly
-- ignored, so the rejection is on the record.
transformation register_customer(customer_id):
    require not Customer(customer_id)
    admit Customer(customer_id)

-- Ask for a screening of a customer against a specific list (sanctions,
-- PEP, and so on). This does not produce a result on the spot - the
-- emitted message carries the request out to the external screening
-- provider, and the answer comes back later through one of the two
-- "record result" steps below.
transformation request_screening(screening_id, customer, list_type, requested_on):
    require Customer(customer)
    admit Screening(screening_id, customer, list_type, requested_on)
    emit ScreeningRequested(screening_id, customer, list_type)

-- The provider came back clean. Record the clean result and make this
-- the current screening for that customer and list, replacing whatever
-- was current before.
transformation record_clean_screening_result(screening_id, completed_on, expires_on):
    bind Screening(screening_id, customer, list_type, _)
    admit ScreeningResult(screening_id, #clean, completed_on, expires_on)
    retract CurrentScreening(customer, list_type, _)
    admit CurrentScreening(customer, list_type, screening_id)

-- The provider came back with a possible match. Record the result and
-- flag it for an analyst to review. Crucially, this does NOT become the
-- current screening - a screening with an unresolved match cannot stand
-- as the customer's current result until a human has settled it. The
-- emitted message routes the match to the review queue.
transformation record_match_screening_result(screening_id, completed_on, expires_on, raised_on):
    bind Screening(screening_id, customer, list_type, _)
    admit ScreeningResult(screening_id, #match, completed_on, expires_on)
    admit MatchUnderReview(screening_id, raised_on)
    emit MatchRaised(screening_id, customer, list_type)

-- An analyst decides a flagged match was a false positive - the names
-- collided, but it is not the same person. The match flag is cleared and
-- this screening becomes the current result. (A confirmed true match
-- would instead lead to rejecting the customer; that path is not modelled
-- here.)
transformation adjudicate_match_as_false_positive(screening_id, adjudicated_on, expires_on):
    require MatchUnderReview(screening_id, _)
    bind Screening(screening_id, customer, list_type, _)
    admit ScreeningResult(screening_id, #adjudicated_clear, adjudicated_on, expires_on)
    retract MatchUnderReview(screening_id, _)
    retract CurrentScreening(customer, list_type, _)
    admit CurrentScreening(customer, list_type, screening_id)

-- Onboard the customer - open the account. This step itself only checks
-- that the customer exists and is not already onboarded; the heavy
-- lifting is left to the invariants. They demand current clean
-- screenings on both the sanctions and PEP lists and no unresolved match
-- anywhere. If any of that fails, the whole onboarding is refused and
-- nothing is committed.
transformation onboard_customer(customer, onboarded_on):
    require Customer(customer)
    require not OnboardedCustomer(customer, _)
    admit OnboardedCustomer(customer, onboarded_on)
    emit CustomerOnboarded(customer, onboarded_on)

-- Reject a customer outright - say, a confirmed sanctions hit or a
-- prohibited jurisdiction. Nothing is removed: the customer record stays
-- on file with the rejection alongside it, so the decision and its
-- reason survive in the audit trail. Reapplying would be a separate
-- process.
transformation reject_customer(customer, reason):
    require Customer(customer)
    emit CustomerRejected(customer, reason)
